const title = "Traeger Error Codes & Not Heating — Same Day LA";
const description = "Traeger error codes explained: AC letter codes (LEr, HEr, Er1-Er7) and WiFIRE numeric codes (0001-0023), plus why a Traeger won't heat up. Operator checks vs technician. $89 outdoor.";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "Why is my Traeger not heating up?",
    a: "Traeger's own number-one cause of temperature problems is poor or wet pellet quality, so start there: empty the hopper, vacuum out any sawdust, and refill with fresh, dry pellets. Then check the firepot for ash buildup (ash smothers the hot rod), confirm nothing is sitting within about an inch of the barrel walls or blocking the drip tray, and make sure you used a closed-lid startup. If it still won't reach temperature after that, the likely part is the hot rod (igniter), the induction/combustion fan, the auger motor, or the RTD temperature probe — those are what our techs replace. $89 outdoor diagnostic, waived with repair."
  },
  {
    q: "What does LEr mean on my Traeger?",
    a: "LEr is the Low Temperature error on AC (non-WiFIRE) Traeger grills: the grill dropped below 125°F for more than 10 minutes, so it shut down the auger, fan, and igniter as a safety. Common causes are an improper open-lid startup, wet or poor-quality pellets, cold or windy weather, an empty firepot, a failed hot rod, or a fouled RTD probe. Start with fresh pellets and a clean firepot; if LEr returns, the hot rod or RTD is the usual culprit. On WiFIRE (touchscreen/app) grills the same condition shows as error 0002 / ER02 instead."
  },
  {
    q: "What does error 0002 or ER02 mean on my WiFIRE Traeger?",
    a: "0002 (also shown as ER02) is the Low Temp Error on WiFIRE and D2 Traeger grills: the grill fell below 120°F for more than 10 minutes. Traeger's fixes are to use a proper closed-lid startup, check the hopper pellet level, confirm the firmware is current, and replace poor or wet pellets. It is the WiFIRE equivalent of the LEr code on older AC grills. If it keeps coming back after fresh pellets and a clean firepot, the hot rod, fan, or thermocouple needs a technician."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I clear a Traeger auger jam?",
    a: "On a WiFIRE grill, use the menu: MENU then AUGER then CLEAR AUGER. On an AC grill, hold the DOWN and IGNITE buttons together in Idle mode for about 60 seconds to cycle the auger. Also empty the hopper and vacuum out sawdust, since damp pellet dust is the usual cause of a jam. If the auger still won't turn, or the error (Er6/Er7 on AC, 0013 on WiFIRE) returns, the auger motor needs replacement — that is a technician job, not an operator fix."
  },
  {
    q: "I saw a Traeger code online that isn't on this page — what is it?",
    a: "We only publish codes that appear in Traeger's own support documentation. Some codes floating around forums and parts blogs (for example LEG, GEr, ErR, or ErH) are not in Traeger's official error database, and their claimed meanings conflict with Traeger's real codes — so we don't guess at them. If your Traeger shows something you can't match here, don't chase an unverified meaning: send us the exact code and your model, and our techs will read it against Traeger's service references. $89 outdoor diagnostic, waived with repair."
  }
];

